{
  "semantics": {
    "attributes": {
      "final": {},
      "header": {}
    },
    "blocks": [
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "An application can send messages that target a specific user, and Spring’s STOMP support recognizes destinations prefixed with "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "/user/"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " for this purpose. For example, a client might subscribe to the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "/user/queue/position-updates"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " destination. "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "UserDestinationMessageHandler"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " handles this destination and transforms it into a destination unique to the user session (such as "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "/queue/position-updates-user123"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "). This provides the convenience of subscribing to a generically named destination while, at the same time, ensuring no collisions with other users who subscribe to the same destination so that each user can receive unique stock position updates."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "admonition",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "When working with user destinations, it is important to configure broker and application destination prefixes as shown in "
          },
          {
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Enable STOMP"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "xref",
            "target": "web/websocket/stomp/enable.html"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ", or otherwise the broker would handle \"/user\" prefixed messages that should only be handled by "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "UserDestinationMessageHandler"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null,
        "type": "tip"
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "On the sending side, messages can be sent to a destination such as "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "/user/{username}/queue/position-updates"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ", which in turn is translated by the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "UserDestinationMessageHandler"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " into one or more destinations, one for each session associated with the user. This lets any component within the application send messages that target a specific user without necessarily knowing anything more than their name and the generic destination. This is also supported through an annotation and a messaging template."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "A message-handling method can send messages to the user associated with the message being handled through the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@SendToUser"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " annotation (also supported on the class-level to share a common destination), as the following example shows:"
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "listing",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "@Controller\npublic class PortfolioController {\n\n\t@MessageMapping(\"/trade\")\n\t@SendToUser(\"/queue/position-updates\")\n\tpublic TradeResult executeTrade(Trade trade, Principal principal) {\n\t\t// ...\n\t\treturn tradeResult;\n\t}\n}"
          }
        ],
        "language": "java",
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "If the user has more than one session, by default, all of the sessions subscribed to the given destination are targeted. However, sometimes, it may be necessary to target only the session that sent the message being handled. You can do so by setting the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "broadcast"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " attribute to false, as the following example shows:"
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "listing",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "@Controller\npublic class MyController {\n\n\t@MessageMapping(\"/action\")\n\tpublic void handleAction() throws Exception{\n\t\t// raise MyBusinessException here\n\t}\n\n\t@MessageExceptionHandler\n\t@SendToUser(destinations=\"/queue/errors\", broadcast=false)\n\tpublic ApplicationError handleException(MyBusinessException exception) {\n\t\t// ...\n\t\treturn appError;\n\t}\n}"
          }
        ],
        "language": "java",
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "admonition",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "While user destinations generally imply an authenticated user, it is not strictly required. A WebSocket session that is not associated with an authenticated user can subscribe to a user destination. In such cases, the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@SendToUser"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " annotation behaves exactly the same as with "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "broadcast=false"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " (that is, targeting only the session that sent the message being handled)."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null,
        "type": "note"
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "You can send a message to user destinations from any application component by, for example, injecting the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "SimpMessagingTemplate"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " created by the Java configuration or the XML namespace. (The bean name is "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "brokerMessagingTemplate"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " if required for qualification with "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@Qualifier"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ".) The following example shows how to do so:"
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "listing",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "@Service\npublic class TradeServiceImpl implements TradeService {\n\n\tprivate final SimpMessagingTemplate messagingTemplate;\n\n\t@Autowired\n\tpublic TradeServiceImpl(SimpMessagingTemplate messagingTemplate) {\n\t\tthis.messagingTemplate = messagingTemplate;\n\t}\n\n\t// ...\n\n\tpublic void afterTradeExecuted(Trade trade) {\n\t\tthis.messagingTemplate.convertAndSendToUser(\n\t\t\t\ttrade.getUserName(), \"/queue/position-updates\", trade.getResult());\n\t}\n}"
          }
        ],
        "language": "java",
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "admonition",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "When you use user destinations with an external message broker, you should check the broker documentation on how to manage inactive queues, so that, when the user session is over, all unique user queues are removed. For example, RabbitMQ creates auto-delete queues when you use destinations such as "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "/exchange/amq.direct/position-updates"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ". So, in that case, the client could subscribe to "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "/user/exchange/amq.direct/position-updates"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ". Similarly, ActiveMQ has "
          },
          {
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "configuration options"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "link",
            "target": "https://activemq.apache.org/delete-inactive-destinations.html"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " for purging inactive destinations."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null,
        "type": "note"
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "In a multi-application server scenario, a user destination may remain unresolved because the user is connected to a different server. In such cases, you can configure a destination to broadcast unresolved messages so that other servers have a chance to try. This can be done through the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "userDestinationBroadcast"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " property of the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "MessageBrokerRegistry"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " in Java configuration and the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "user-destination-broadcast"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " attribute of the "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "message-broker"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " element in XML."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      }
    ],
    "outline": null,
    "title": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "value": "User Destinations"
      }
    ]
  }
}
